WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:07.000
The professors were . . . It was like I didn’t exist.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.980
One woman was particularly awful.

3
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.660
She was an older woman.

4
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.340
She announced my first day that . . . not to me,

5
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:20.760
she never . . . People talked about me as though, as I said, I was an inanimate object.

6
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:27.200
She said to her class this was the first time she had ever taught a nigra. [chuckles] I was shocked.

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.660
And she just didn’t know how she was going to get through this experience.

8
00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.880
She taught me math.

9
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.560
Nobody sat near me.

10
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.560
The kids sat all around me.

11
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.660
If I sat in the corner, they would move to the opposite side of the room.

12
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.760
If I sat in the middle, they would move to the sides.

13
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.760
I never sat in the same place or whatever.

14
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.440
But it . . . You were just playing head trips all the time.

15
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.400
You were trying to figure out how you were going to make today work.

16
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.160
It got to be very, very lonely and depressing.

17
00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.100
We were playing racquetball and you had to have a partner.

18
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:08.520
I remember this guy . . . If they didn’t . . . If the professor or the teacher didn’t mention my name,

19
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:14.200
many of them didn’t know who I was because there were a lot of international students at LSU,

20
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.160
and many of them looked more swarthy than I did.

21
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.640
Of course, with your hair all shaved off you couldn’t tell too much anyway.

22
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:30.460
I remember this guy standing next to me saying, “I’ve been looking around here waiting for that nigger to come in and I don’t see him.

23
00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.220
I hope I don’t get him as my partner.

24
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.960
My friends will give me a hard time.”

25
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.820
I said, “Well, there’re a few niggers here, but they’re not black.”

26
00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.080
He didn’t know what I was talking about. [Tureaud and Wallace laugh] He ended up getting me,

27
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.780
and I don’t know if he ever heard the name.

28
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:51.940
I didn’t . . . I wasn’t going to say, “Well, I’m the black person here.”

29
00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.440
I just thought, “Goodness gracious,

30
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.700
how long is it going to take for this to . . . for them to get over this thing?” . . .

31
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:08.020
I particularly remember the women who worked in the cafeteria where I ate.

32
00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.640
They were very quiet, but they were very supportive.

33
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.140
They would smile or wink at you.

34
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.160
Some of them would actually come up and say, “We’re very happy you’re here.”

35
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.160
But quietly, they didn’t want any of the white staff to hear it or the white kids to hear it,

36
00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:31.260
I guess . . . But they were proud and very supportive.

37
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:43.080
My most touching moment—I don’t know if I can say this because it chokes me up but—my most touching moment at LSU,

38
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:51.260
and I’ll never forget it was . . . this . . . I told you . . . I mentioned this to you once before.

39
00:02:51.260 --> 00:03:00.420
One morning, I came out . . . I couldn’t sleep and I came out just to be outside and to get away from that crazy dormitory with all the noise and stuff.

40
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.940
It was like six o’clock in the morning, and I was coming out of the dorm.

41
00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:09.180
I don’t know where I was going, but there was this black man out there sitting in a truck.

42
00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.120
He had on bib overalls and he had a little boy with him.

43
00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.880
As I came out the entrance,

44
00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:22.100
he walked over to me and . . . big smile on his face and he said . . . He asked me if I was A. P. Tureaud.

45
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:22.920
I said, “Yes.”

46
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.820
He said, “I thought you were.”

47
00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:30.240
He said that he had been to the campus a few times, but he hadn’t seen me.

48
00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.060
He lived somewhere in the Baton Rouge area,

49
00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:45.040
and he had brought his son who was six or seven years old to meet me because he wanted his kid to remember that this was possible.

50
00:03:45.040 --> 00:04:01.340
That also made me realize that I had a responsibility to help other people realize that what I was doing was possible for everyone.

51
00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:07.300
Because I didn’t go into this thing with a mission, and I wasn’t there to be a pioneer.

52
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.920
I just wanted to go to school.

53
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.680
But I realized . . . And I knew that would happen;

54
00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:21.780
that it would take on a different meaning for black people because people had come up to me and at home,

55
00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:25.740
people would say, “We’re very proud of you,” and things like that,

56
00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:34.460
but this was a . . . This man and this little boy . . . That just meant so much to me.

57
00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:39.160
It put more responsibility on me because I was trying to figure out how to get out of there.

58
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.900
I mean, the experience was so bad.

59
00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:46.020
But then, I thought, “Well, I can’t leave because I’ll be disappointing everyone.”

60
00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.560
It put me in a more . . . It created conflict for me.

61
00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.280
I then said to myself, “Well I just can’t leave.”

62
00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.040
My folks had told me I could leave any time I wanted to.

63
00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.940
My father said, “You are a person.

64
00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.400
You’re an individual.

65
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.140
Yes, the case is important, but you’re not a case; you’re a person.

66
00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:13.360
We love you, and if it gets too rough, you just say you want out, and we’ll get you out.”

67
00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.400
I just couldn’t figure that . . . I couldn’t do that.

68
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.580
I figured I don’t know how I’m going to be able to take it.

69
00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:25.960
I then started to feel that I was in a conflictual situation that I couldn’t resolve.

70
00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:28.340
I just said, “Time will tell.

71
00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.040
Something’s got to get better.

72
00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.069
Something’s got to change, but I shouldn’t go.

73
00:05:32.069 --> 00:05:37.120
It sure would be nice to be at Xavier University in New Orleans with all my friends,

74
00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.340
enjoying themselves, working hard but having a good time, and not be under all this pressure.”
